        numerical check at y =  0.065: analytic   -1.2781   finite-difference on the exact inversion   -1.2781   (frac diff 8.87e-06)
        numerical check at y =  0.200: analytic   -1.5221   finite-difference on the exact inversion   -1.5221   (frac diff 2.17e-05)
        numerical check at y =  1.000: analytic   -2.4366   finite-difference on the exact inversion   -2.4369   (frac diff 1.23e-04)
        numerical check at y =  2.000: analytic   -3.4028   finite-difference on the exact inversion   -3.4040   (frac diff 3.51e-04)
        numerical check at y =  3.000: analytic   -4.3719   finite-difference on the exact inversion   -4.3752   (frac diff 7.38e-04)
        numerical check at y =  5.000: analytic   -6.4743   finite-difference on the exact inversion   -6.4890   (frac diff 2.28e-03)
        numerical check at y = 10.000: analytic  -13.3089   finite-difference on the exact inversion  -13.5351   (frac diff 1.70e-02)
  [PASS] k02-0 the amplification law is DERIVED and then verified against a finite difference on the exact kernel inversion across three decades in y -- if the analytic form were wrong this fails   (max fractional |analytic - numerical| = 1.70e-02 over the 8 values that invert)

    THE HARD EDGE.  The inversion exists only while g_obs > g_bar, i.e. while the coherent baryonic-mass
    error d (dex) stays below the boost the kernel supplies at that acceleration: d < log10 nu(y).
           y   boost log10 nu(y) (dex)   max tolerable mass error   |LAMBDA|
       0.065                    0.6477                     0.6477       1.28
       0.200                    0.4430                     0.4430       1.52
       1.000                    0.1992                     0.1992       2.44
       2.000                    0.1210                     0.1210       3.40
       3.000                    0.0846                     0.0846       4.37
       5.000                    0.0491                     0.0491       6.47
      10.000                    0.0188                     0.0188      13.31
      20.000                    0.0050                     0.0050      37.70
  [PASS] k02-0b AND THE HARD EDGE IS ITSELF A RESULT: the inversion exists only while the coherent baryonic-mass error is smaller than the boost, d < log10 nu(y).  At y = 10 that budget is 0.018 dex -- four percent in stellar mass -- so a survey observing at y >~ 5 cannot report a_0 at all, only a lower limit   (at y = 10 the whole budget is 0.0188 dex and a 0.05 dex mass error returns nan; the y with a 0.13 dex budget (the decisive test's own target) is 1.83)

