==========================================================================================================================
    survey                            N    <z>   median y            16-84% y   LAMBDA(median y)  <LAMBDA> per obj
    SPARC at R_e (z~0)              129   0.00      0.186       0.060 - 1.639              -1.50             -1.50
    SPARC last point (z~0)          147   0.00      0.062       0.030 - 0.163              -1.27             -1.27
    RC100 (z 0.6-2.5)               100   1.59      1.959       0.889 - 4.499              -3.36             -3.36
    MSA-3D golden (z 0.6-1.7)        23   1.12      1.039       0.369 - 1.694              -2.48             -2.48
    KROSS stars-only (z~0.9)        381   0.88      1.884       0.607 - 7.209              -3.29             -3.29
    MUSE-DARK II lensed (z 0.5-1.45)   95   0.98      0.330       0.096 - 1.442              -1.70             -1.70
  [PASS] k02-1 THE STRUCTURAL FINDING, and it is against the whole redshift front: the high-redshift surveys sit one to two decades higher in y than the local rotation curves that calibrate a_0, so their stellar-mass lever is several times larger.  a_0 is not measured at high z on the same footing as at z = 0   (SPARC last point median y = 0.062, LAMBDA = -1.27;  RC100 median y = 1.959, LAMBDA = -3.36;  MSA-3D LAMBDA = -2.48)

    d log10 y / dz across the two high-z surveys = +0.179 per unit z (y at z=0.6: 1.17; at z=2.5: 2.56)
    => |LAMBDA| at z=0.6 is 2.60 and at z=2.5 is 3.94: the measurement gets HARDER exactly where the decisive test lives.

