    (the SAMPLE-median lever differs from the per-object one because objects near y ~ 5 drop out of the
     inversion when g_bar is raised -- the k02-0b breakdown showing up inside real data.)

==========================================================================================================================
PART 3 -- the diagnosis: what stellar-mass offset does the measured survey-to-survey a_0 gap correspond to?
==========================================================================================================================
    median a_0: SPARC(R_e) 1.164e-10   RC100 1.366e-10   MSA-3D 3.513e-10
    MSA-3D minus RC100 = +0.410 dex  (item 101 measured +0.404 dex on the same closed form)
    the two surveys' SELF-CONSISTENT levers (LAMBDA at each survey's own fitted a_0): RC100 -2.77, MSA-3D -1.63
    solved on the real data: raising MSA-3D's baryonic masses by +0.091 dex and lowering RC100's by the same amount (+0.182 dex relative) makes the two surveys' a_0 AGREE EXACTLY (residual +0.0000 dex)
    at SPARC's own LAMBDA = -1.27 the same 0.182 dex mass difference would produce only 0.231 dex of a_0.
  [PASS] k02-3 THE DIAGNOSIS, and it is quantitative: the 0.4 dex survey-to-survey a_0 gap that item 101 could only report is what a baryonic-mass calibration difference of about 0.1 dex each way -- ordinary between two SED pipelines with different IMF, SFH and gas treatments -- produces once the high-z amplification is applied.  Nothing about a_0 is required, and this check fails if the required offset is implausible   (required relative offset +0.182 dex (+0.091 each way), against a typical SED-code-to-SED-code systematic of 0.1-0.3 dex; verified by solving on the real g_bar and g_obs)

