    THE CAVEAT that sizes it, carried from item 16c: inside RC100 corr(log a_0, f_DM) = +0.735, so this slope is
    a monotone restatement of that survey's own falling dark-matter fractions and inherits their systematics.
  [PASS] M5 the z-shuffle mutation fires: permuting redshift WITHIN each survey destroys the slope, so what is measured is a redshift trend inside the surveys and not the two surveys' different levels   (real -0.0659, shuffled -0.0026 +- 0.0683)
  [PASS] k02-5 the joint high-z slope done with a free level per survey (the only defensible combination once the levels disagree by 0.4 dex) is consistent with a CONSTANT a_0 and does not separate the rivals; it is reported beside the naive stacked slope, which is the survey ladder read as a line   (per-survey-level slope -0.0659 +- 0.0674 dex/z (0.98s from flat); naive stacked -0.1552 +- 0.0684)

==========================================================================================================================
PART 6 -- the design rule the law gives for the decisive z ~ 2.5 measurement
==========================================================================================================================
    Target: a_0 at z ~ 2.5 to +-0.13 dex (framework 0.00 against a LambdaCDM-native +0.33).
    stellar-mass systematic       |LAMBDA| allowed    y required   R/R_e required*
          0.02 dex                            6.50        5.0227              0.62
          0.05 dex                            2.60        1.1651              1.30
          0.10 dex                            1.30        0.0747              5.12
          0.13 dex                            1.00    IMPOSSIBLE                --
          0.15 dex                            0.87    IMPOSSIBLE                --
          0.20 dex                            0.65    IMPOSSIBLE                --
          0.30 dex                            0.43    IMPOSSIBLE                --

    A HARD FLOOR falls out of the same law and it is worth stating on its own: |LAMBDA(y)| >= 1 for every y,
    with equality only in the deep-MOND limit.  So a_0 can NEVER be measured to better precision than the
    baryonic mass scale it is read against -- at any acceleration, in any survey, at any redshift.
  [PASS] k02-6 the hard floor, proved numerically over five decades in y: |LAMBDA| >= 1 everywhere, so no a_0 measurement can beat its own baryonic-mass calibration.  The decisive +-0.13 dex test therefore REQUIRES the baryonic mass at z ~ 2.5 to better than 0.13 dex, whatever else is done   (min |LAMBDA| over y = 1e-3 to 100 is 1.010033, attained at y = 1.00e-04)
    *R/R_e for a point-mass baryon field, using RC100's own median y(R_e); a real disc needs a little more.
    Read plainly: with a 0.15 dex stellar-mass systematic the decisive test needs the rotation curve measured
    at roughly 3-5 effective radii, not at one -- which is exactly the regime no z ~ 2.5 survey reaches today.

