PART 7 -- mutation controls
==========================================================================================================================
    (the common a_0 the two surveys share once corrected: 2.788e-10 = +0.47 dex from canonical, +0.39 from alt)
  [PASS] M1 the mechanism, injected and recovered: two synthetic surveys built to obey ONE a_0 exactly at their own real g_bar values, differing only by the solved baryonic-mass offset, reproduce the observed level gap to within 15% -- so the gap needs no a_0 physics   (a relative mass error of +0.182 dex on synthetic one-a_0 data reproduces +0.351 dex against the observed +0.410 dex)
  [PASS] M2 closure: with NO offset the same two synthetic surveys agree exactly, so the gap in M1 is the offset and not the different y-distributions of the two samples   (no-offset synthetic gap +0.0000 dex)
    footing canonical : RC100 median y = 1.959, median LAMBDA = -3.36
    footing alt       : RC100 median y = 1.623, median LAMBDA = -3.04
  [PASS] M3 both footings: the conclusion is footing-independent -- the high-z samples sit at y ~ 1-2 on either footing and the amplification is 3-4 either way   (canonical LAMBDA -3.36, alt -3.04)
  [PASS] M4 kernel swap AGAINST INTEREST: the amplification is not special to Route A -- the 'simple' interpolation function gives the same law with a similar value at y = 2, so this is a property of ANY interpolating kernel measured out of the deep regime and cannot be escaped by changing nu   (Route A LAMBDA(2) = -3.40; simple-nu LAMBDA(2) = -3.73)

==========================================================================================================================
PART 8 -- the LambdaCDM alternative computed beside the framework
==========================================================================================================================
  LambdaCDM reads the same numbers as f_DM(<R_e), not as a_0.  The amplification is then absent by construction: f_DM is linear in g_bar, so d f_DM/d ln Upsilon = -(1-f_DM), a lever of order 0.3-0.7,
  not 3-5.  That asymmetry is real and it is against the framework: the SAME data support a much more stable statement in LambdaCDM's variable than in the framework's.  The framework's compensation is that
  its variable is supposed to be a CONSTANT, so its scatter is a test and f_DM's is not.
    RC100: d f_DM/d ln Upsilon has median -0.710 against the framework's d ln a_0/d ln Upsilon = -3.36
    sd(log a_0) inside RC100 = 0.411 dex; sd(f_DM) = 0.209

==========================================================================================================================
VERDICT (k02)
==========================================================================================================================
  LAMBDA(y) = (1 + m)/m is exact, verified numerically, and confronted survey by survey.  It says:
   * SPARC's outer points sit at y = 0.062, LAMBDA = -1.27;  RC100 at R_e sits at y = 1.96, LAMBDA = -3.36.
   * the 0.4 dex RC100-vs-MSA-3D gap is what a +0.18 dex baryonic-mass difference produces.  It is
     not evidence about a_0 and must never be quoted as such.
   * y RISES with redshift at d log10 y/dz = +0.179, so |LAMBDA| runs from 2.6 at z = 0.6
     to 3.9 at z = 2.5: the amplification is worst exactly where the decisive test lives.
  This is a THEOREM ABOUT MEASUREMENT derived from the RAR, not an independent second law.  Its product is
  the design rule in Part 6 and the retirement of every existing high-z a_0 'level' as a measurement.
